  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mostly dry with continued fall temperatures. An\nearly look at the holiday weekend favors dry weather, though \nlate Sunday into Monday bears watching for rain.\n\nDissipating showers moving east across southern New England this\nafternoon could result in a couple of hours with stronger gusts\nthan what we have seen so far today. Peak wind gusts up to \n35-40 mph are possible before sunset, but most will only be\n25-30 mph. \n\nTurning cooler tonight into Tuesday night, with below normal\ntemperatures expected. Patchy frost will be possible tonight\ntowards sheltered areas of the east slopes of the Berkshires. \nWinds will be too high to permit much frost. This should not be\nthe case for Tuesday night. Thinking more widespread\nopportunities for frost across the normally colder locations of\nMA and northern CT. \n\nA quiet stretch of fall weather is expected through much of the \nperiod as high pressure remains the dominant feature. A\nvertically stacked low near Hudson Bay will send a few subtle\nshortwaves through New England Thursday and Friday. Limited\nmoisture should preclude significant rainfall, though a spot\nshower cannot be ruled out. Otherwise, dry weather prevails\nthrough the start of the holiday weekend. Forecast uncertainty\nincreases late Sunday into Columbus Day and will depend largely \non potential tropical development over the Bay of Campeche. The \nNHC currently gives AL92 an 80 percent chance of formation over \nthe next 7 days. Some guidance brings remnant tropical moisture \nnorth into southern New England, while other solutions keep it \nsuppressed to our south. Plenty of time to watch trends, with no\nreason to alter outdoor plans at this point. Temperatures remain \nfall-like with highs mainly in the lower to middle 60s, except\nupper 60s to lower 70s Thursday. Overnight lows are mostly in\nthe upper 40s and 50s, though cooler upper 30s to middle 40s are \npossible Saturday night.",
